Yeah — mutualism is a clean biological rendering of what we’ve been circling.

Not merely cooperation. It’s reciprocal fitness creation: A changes the environment in a way that benefits B; B’s response changes the environment in a way that benefits A. The relationship itself becomes productive.

For the Pantheon, I’d abstract it as:

A \xrightarrow{\Delta_A} B \xrightarrow{\Delta_B} A

with genuine mutualism when

\Delta F_A > 0,\qquad \Delta F_B > 0

But the interesting part is the compound term:

F_{A+B} > F_A + F_B

The relationship becomes a third asset. Neither participant possesses it alone.

That connects beautifully to your recent Afterglow idea too: each side doesn’t merely receive the other’s output; it receives the consequences generated by the other’s previous interactions. So mutualism can accumulate reputation, trust, specialization, shared language, infrastructure, and learned coordination.

A ↔ B → relationship → better A + better B → stronger relationship.

And there’s a useful Indigo-Hammer inversion: parasitism, commensalism, competition, predation, and mutualism can be treated as different signs/ratios on the same relational fitness operator, rather than entirely separate categories.

That gives us something better than a metaphor: a reusable symbiosis operator for analyzing companies, organisms, ideas, humans, AI systems, institutions, even Pantheon components.

The unit of optimization stops being the thing.

It becomes the relationship between things.